WEBVTT

00:00.090 --> 00:00.923
Instructor: So next up,

00:00.923 --> 00:03.540
we're gonna talk about the Objects Folder.

00:03.540 --> 00:06.420
Now, this folder we're gonna spend quite a lot of time

00:06.420 --> 00:09.000
discussing over a couple of videos.

00:09.000 --> 00:11.550
This is really the core of Git.

00:11.550 --> 00:15.150
This is where all of the backups of our files,

00:15.150 --> 00:18.090
all the content is stored, all the commits live here.

00:18.090 --> 00:21.450
And the way that it works is a lot to explain,

00:21.450 --> 00:24.780
but it's also interesting and I think, hopefully,

00:24.780 --> 00:27.450
if I succeed, this section will make sense.

00:27.450 --> 00:29.820
This is the important stuff I want to cover in this section.

00:29.820 --> 00:31.980
It all has to do with hashing,

00:31.980 --> 00:35.400
and we're gonna talk about things like blobs and trees.

00:35.400 --> 00:37.140
Anyway, we'll get into that.

00:37.140 --> 00:40.080
But in this video, I just wanna show you, it exists.

00:40.080 --> 00:41.400
There is a folder, right?

00:41.400 --> 00:43.710
If we look in the ".git" directory,

00:43.710 --> 00:46.560
if I can spell it, of course, called "objects."

00:46.560 --> 00:49.920
And if we look inside of it, it's right here.

00:49.920 --> 00:51.540
So let me close down HEAD.

00:51.540 --> 00:54.180
Objects here has a bunch of sub directories

00:54.180 --> 00:56.220
at least on my repository.

00:56.220 --> 00:58.140
I'm using this React example.

00:58.140 --> 01:01.350
So if you have a repo and it has some history,

01:01.350 --> 01:02.730
you'll have something in here,

01:02.730 --> 01:07.320
but probably not the same combination, the same digits.

01:07.320 --> 01:09.180
But anyway, we have these two digit

01:09.180 --> 01:11.340
hexadecimal folder names.

01:11.340 --> 01:14.340
And then inside of them there are files.

01:14.340 --> 01:16.740
But then it tells me, "Hmm, I can't display it for you.

01:16.740 --> 01:20.370
It's either binary or an unsupported text encoding type."

01:20.370 --> 01:23.670
So we're not gonna be able to look at the contents.

01:23.670 --> 01:26.910
This is what it does when it tries to display in VS code.

01:26.910 --> 01:30.150
These are compressed, encrypted binary files.

01:30.150 --> 01:32.490
So they're not really meant for us to look at at all.

01:32.490 --> 01:34.530
They're meant to be small.

01:34.530 --> 01:36.840
But they store, those files store,

01:36.840 --> 01:39.750
all the Git history, all the commits,

01:39.750 --> 01:41.970
all of the files and their information,

01:41.970 --> 01:43.860
the contents of files.

01:43.860 --> 01:46.740
Git stores full snapshots.

01:46.740 --> 01:50.760
It does not store Diffs or changes

01:50.760 --> 01:53.310
like a Delta between two commits.

01:53.310 --> 01:57.150
It stores full complete snapshots, which is somewhat unique.

01:57.150 --> 01:58.650
So we'll talk about that.

01:58.650 --> 01:59.940
But inside of this folder,

01:59.940 --> 02:03.480
there are really four different types of basic Git objects,

02:03.480 --> 02:04.440
and this is what we're gonna spend

02:04.440 --> 02:06.840
a lot of time talking about in the next few videos.

02:06.840 --> 02:09.750
Those four types are commits, trees,

02:09.750 --> 02:12.660
blobs, and annotated tags.

02:12.660 --> 02:14.580
So these are four different things

02:14.580 --> 02:16.980
that are stored in this Objects directory.

02:16.980 --> 02:18.960
Really the core of Git.

02:18.960 --> 02:21.390
They're all going to be hashed.

02:21.390 --> 02:23.550
We've seen the commit hashes before,

02:23.550 --> 02:25.320
those long, crazy strings.

02:25.320 --> 02:27.420
We're going to learn a lot more about those

02:27.420 --> 02:29.340
in the next couple of videos.

02:29.340 --> 02:30.990
So where does this come from?

02:30.990 --> 02:32.640
What does this mean?

02:32.640 --> 02:35.670
Not the contents, but what do these file names mean,

02:35.670 --> 02:37.020
and these folder names?

02:37.020 --> 02:39.600
Those are mysteries that will hopefully be explained soon,

02:39.600 --> 02:41.370
if I do my job.

02:41.370 --> 02:42.840
I just wanted to introduce that folder.

02:42.840 --> 02:44.730
So there it is, the Objects directory.

02:44.730 --> 02:47.940
It contains all the data in a repository.

02:47.940 --> 02:49.290
So in the next video,

02:49.290 --> 02:51.513
we're gonna start by talking about hashing.
